1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,850
Luzon, the Philippines.

2
00:00:01,870 --> 00:00:05,357
The thirtieth of January, 1945,

3
00:00:05,377 --> 00:00:07,958
twenty minutes to seven in the evening.

4
00:00:07,978 --> 00:00:09,816
A twin-engined American night fighter,

5
00:00:09,836 --> 00:00:13,160
a P-61 Black Widow, comes in low over a

6
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:17,130
prison camp near the town of Cabanatuan.

7
00:00:17,150 --> 00:00:20,002
It crosses the camp twice at about two

8
00:00:20,022 --> 00:00:20,719
hundred feet.

9
00:00:20,739 --> 00:00:23,232
On the ground, every Japanese guard is

10
00:00:23,252 --> 00:00:24,052
looking up.

11
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:28,075
Nobody is looking at the field in front of

12
00:00:28,095 --> 00:00:28,718
the gate.

13
00:00:28,738 --> 00:00:31,292
Out there, in the last of the light,

14
00:00:31,312 --> 00:00:33,543
more than a hundred American soldiers are

15
00:00:33,563 --> 00:00:35,152
lying flat in the grass.

16
00:00:35,172 --> 00:00:37,403
They are Rangers, and they have been

17
00:00:37,423 --> 00:00:40,298
crawling for over an hour across a mile of

18
00:00:40,318 --> 00:00:42,550
open rice land with almost no cover.

19
00:00:42,570 --> 00:00:45,123
One guard in a tower raises his rifle

20
00:00:45,143 --> 00:00:47,053
toward the ditch where they lie.

21
00:00:47,073 --> 00:00:48,360
Then he lowers it.

22
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:52,312
Inside the wire are more than five hundred

23
00:00:52,332 --> 00:00:53,374
prisoners of war.

24
00:00:53,394 --> 00:00:55,852
Many of them have been prisoners since

25
00:00:55,872 --> 00:01:00,120
Bataan and Corregidor fell in 1942.

26
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:01,171
Most are sick.

27
00:01:01,191 --> 00:01:02,223
Some cannot walk.

28
00:01:02,243 --> 00:01:05,377
At a quarter to eight, a single rifle shot

29
00:01:05,397 --> 00:01:08,180
cracks through the dark, and the camp erupts.

30
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:10,633
Two weeks later, one of those prisoners,

31
00:01:10,653 --> 00:01:13,086
his name not recorded on the film,

32
00:01:13,106 --> 00:01:16,260
told an Army camera what that moment was like.

33
00:01:18,650 --> 00:01:21,585
Shortly after dark, I decided to turn in

34
00:01:21,605 --> 00:01:22,694
my bamboo bunk.

35
00:01:22,714 --> 00:01:26,019
I went in the barracks and sat on the

36
00:01:26,039 --> 00:01:29,344
ledge of the bunk and started to take off

37
00:01:29,364 --> 00:01:30,082
my shoes.

38
00:01:30,102 --> 00:01:32,299
Suddenly a lot of shooting started.

39
00:01:32,319 --> 00:01:34,516
I thought the end had came.

40
00:01:34,536 --> 00:01:38,230
I was under the impression that it was a massacre.

41
00:01:39,450 --> 00:01:41,546
This is the documented story of the raid

42
00:01:41,566 --> 00:01:44,833
at Cabanatuan, the night a hundred and

43
00:01:44,852 --> 00:01:46,948
twenty Rangers of the Sixth Ranger

44
00:01:46,968 --> 00:01:49,063
Battalion, a dozen Alamo Scouts and

45
00:01:49,083 --> 00:01:50,825
hundreds of Filipino guerrillas went

46
00:01:50,845 --> 00:01:52,940
twenty-five miles behind Japanese lines to

47
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:55,408
bring out the last survivors of Bataan.

48
00:01:55,428 --> 00:01:58,228
You will hear it from the Army's own

49
00:01:58,248 --> 00:02:01,310
reports, the newspapers of February 1945,

50
00:02:01,330 --> 00:02:02,690
and the freed men themselves.

51
00:02:04,475 --> 00:02:07,015
To understand that night, you have to

52
00:02:07,035 --> 00:02:08,844
start almost three years earlier.

53
00:02:08,864 --> 00:02:12,392
In April 1942 the American and Filipino

54
00:02:12,412 --> 00:02:13,769
army on Bataan surrendered,

55
00:02:13,789 --> 00:02:16,951
and in May the fortress of Corregidor followed.

56
00:02:16,971 --> 00:02:19,799
The men who survived the march out of

57
00:02:19,819 --> 00:02:21,935
Bataan were packed into Camp O'Donnell.

58
00:02:21,955 --> 00:02:24,783
From there, the Americans were moved to a

59
00:02:24,803 --> 00:02:27,666
camp near Cabanatuan, about five miles

60
00:02:27,686 --> 00:02:31,225
east of the town, at a place called Pangatian.

61
00:02:31,925 --> 00:02:33,813
It was a grim place.

62
00:02:33,833 --> 00:02:36,102
The Marine Corps official history says

63
00:02:36,122 --> 00:02:39,851
that at Cabanatuan, Americans died at a

64
00:02:39,871 --> 00:02:42,031
rate of forty to fifty a day.

65
00:02:42,051 --> 00:02:43,899
It records one date on purpose:

66
00:02:43,919 --> 00:02:46,745
the sixteenth of January, 1943,

67
00:02:46,765 --> 00:02:49,432
the first day in the history of the camp

68
00:02:49,452 --> 00:02:50,328
without a death.

69
00:02:50,348 --> 00:02:53,674
By December 1943, more than two thousand

70
00:02:53,694 --> 00:02:55,720
six hundred Americans were buried in the

71
00:02:55,740 --> 00:02:56,540
camp's cemetery.

72
00:02:57,675 --> 00:02:59,786
The men lived on rice.

73
00:02:59,806 --> 00:03:02,769
A freed prisoner told the Associated Press

74
00:03:02,789 --> 00:03:05,915
in 1945 that they had been given three

75
00:03:05,935 --> 00:03:06,865
handfuls a day.

76
00:03:06,885 --> 00:03:09,082
One of them, a veterinarian named Herbert

77
00:03:09,102 --> 00:03:11,615
Ott, later said they were down to about

78
00:03:11,635 --> 00:03:13,198
eight hundred calories a day,

79
00:03:13,218 --> 00:03:14,782
and still had to work.

80
00:03:14,802 --> 00:03:17,315
It took twelve hundred just to stay alive,

81
00:03:17,335 --> 00:03:20,165
he said, so a lot of men simply worked

82
00:03:20,185 --> 00:03:21,135
themselves to death.

83
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:24,807
The barracks were long huts with thatched

84
00:03:24,827 --> 00:03:26,540
roofs, built for forty men.

85
00:03:26,560 --> 00:03:28,967
The Japanese put about a hundred and

86
00:03:28,987 --> 00:03:30,353
twenty in each one.

87
00:03:30,373 --> 00:03:33,127
At its peak, the camp held some eight

88
00:03:33,147 --> 00:03:33,947
thousand prisoners.

89
00:03:35,575 --> 00:03:38,855
In October 1944, about sixteen hundred

90
00:03:38,875 --> 00:03:42,375
prisoners were marched out of Cabanatuan.

91
00:03:42,395 --> 00:03:44,084
The Japanese were shipping able-bodied

92
00:03:44,104 --> 00:03:45,792
prisoners to Japan to work,

93
00:03:45,812 --> 00:03:48,868
in the holds of cargo ships the men called

94
00:03:48,888 --> 00:03:49,551
hell ships.

95
00:03:49,571 --> 00:03:51,601
In December, more than sixteen hundred

96
00:03:51,621 --> 00:03:53,993
prisoners were loaded aboard one of them,

97
00:03:54,013 --> 00:03:56,455
the Oryoku Maru, in Manila harbor.

98
00:03:57,800 --> 00:04:00,461
Those left behind at Cabanatuan were the

99
00:04:00,481 --> 00:04:02,625
ones the Japanese did not want:

100
00:04:02,645 --> 00:04:04,788
the sick, the blind, the crippled,

101
00:04:04,808 --> 00:04:06,591
men too weak to work.

102
00:04:06,611 --> 00:04:09,836
There were a little over five hundred of them.

103
00:04:09,856 --> 00:04:11,999
Most were Americans, survivors of Bataan

104
00:04:12,019 --> 00:04:14,100
and Corregidor.

105
00:04:14,120 --> 00:04:16,140
There were also British prisoners from

106
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:18,180
Singapore, a few Dutch and Norwegians,

107
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:19,220
and some civilians.

108
00:04:20,675 --> 00:04:23,436
They kept themselves alive in small ways.

109
00:04:23,456 --> 00:04:25,820
An Associated Press reporter wrote that

110
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:28,601
they had collected six thousand recipes in

111
00:04:28,621 --> 00:04:28,998
captivity.

112
00:04:29,018 --> 00:04:32,574
And they had built a radio inside a water

113
00:04:32,594 --> 00:04:35,375
canteen that could pick up San Francisco.

114
00:04:36,775 --> 00:04:40,431
Early in January 1945, the regular guard

115
00:04:40,451 --> 00:04:41,946
company left the camp.

116
00:04:41,966 --> 00:04:44,976
For a few days the prisoners were nearly

117
00:04:44,996 --> 00:04:47,248
alone, with a senior American officer,

118
00:04:47,268 --> 00:04:49,141
Colonel James Duckworth, in charge.

119
00:04:49,161 --> 00:04:51,035
Then Japanese troops came back.

120
00:04:51,055 --> 00:04:53,686
Some of them were soldiers passing through

121
00:04:53,706 --> 00:04:55,201
on their way north.

122
00:04:55,221 --> 00:04:58,230
The prisoners could hear the war getting closer.

123
00:04:58,250 --> 00:05:01,260
They did not know whether it would free

124
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:02,795
them or kill them.

125
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:07,380
On the ninth of January, 1945,

126
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:09,626
General Walter Krueger's Sixth Army landed

127
00:05:09,646 --> 00:05:12,513
at Lingayen Gulf on the west coast of

128
00:05:12,532 --> 00:05:14,744
Luzon, the same beaches the Japanese had

129
00:05:14,764 --> 00:05:18,220
used in December 1941.

130
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:19,999
General MacArthur had come back.

131
00:05:20,019 --> 00:05:22,489
Within three weeks the American front line

132
00:05:22,509 --> 00:05:25,820
had pushed inland to the town of Guimba,

133
00:05:25,840 --> 00:05:28,120
about twenty-five miles from the prison camp.

134
00:05:29,350 --> 00:05:30,671
That was the danger.

135
00:05:30,691 --> 00:05:32,683
When the Distinguished Service Cross was

136
00:05:32,703 --> 00:05:34,695
awarded to the raid's commander three

137
00:05:34,715 --> 00:05:37,042
weeks later, the citation spelled out why

138
00:05:37,062 --> 00:05:38,719
the mission had been ordered:

139
00:05:38,739 --> 00:05:41,401
it was believed that the enemy would kill

140
00:05:41,421 --> 00:05:43,749
or remove the prisoners when the American

141
00:05:43,769 --> 00:05:45,110
attack reached that area.

142
00:05:46,425 --> 00:05:48,886
There was reason to believe it.

143
00:05:48,906 --> 00:05:52,195
A month earlier, on the island of Palawan,

144
00:05:52,215 --> 00:05:55,090
Japanese guards had herded a hundred and

145
00:05:55,110 --> 00:05:57,571
fifty American prisoners into air raid

146
00:05:57,591 --> 00:06:00,052
shelters and set them on fire.

147
00:06:00,072 --> 00:06:00,880
Eleven escaped.

148
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:03,774
A Special Operations history of the raid

149
00:06:03,794 --> 00:06:06,256
records that their accounts were quickly

150
00:06:06,276 --> 00:06:07,910
passed to MacArthur's headquarters.

151
00:06:07,930 --> 00:06:10,805
Whether Palawan was the reason for the

152
00:06:10,825 --> 00:06:13,419
Cabanatuan raid, the sources do not agree.

153
00:06:13,439 --> 00:06:16,642
But no one at Sixth Army could be sure the

154
00:06:16,662 --> 00:06:18,898
prisoners would still be alive when the

155
00:06:18,918 --> 00:06:19,885
front line arrived.

156
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:23,045
On the twenty-sixth of January,

157
00:06:23,065 --> 00:06:25,282
an American who led guerrillas behind

158
00:06:25,302 --> 00:06:27,520
Japanese lines on Luzon, Major Robert

159
00:06:27,540 --> 00:06:29,587
Lapham, came to Sixth Army.

160
00:06:29,607 --> 00:06:32,363
His Filipino runners had been watching the

161
00:06:32,383 --> 00:06:34,743
camp, and he proposed a rescue.

162
00:06:34,763 --> 00:06:36,727
On the recommendation of Krueger's

163
00:06:36,747 --> 00:06:38,710
intelligence chief, Colonel Horton White,

164
00:06:38,730 --> 00:06:41,883
Krueger gave the job to the Sixth Ranger

165
00:06:41,903 --> 00:06:42,703
Battalion.

166
00:06:44,125 --> 00:06:46,924
The Sixth Ranger Battalion had begun life

167
00:06:46,944 --> 00:06:48,535
as an artillery battalion,

168
00:06:48,555 --> 00:06:50,146
the Ninety-Eighth Field Artillery,

169
00:06:50,166 --> 00:06:52,160
activated at Fort Lewis, Washington,

170
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:55,105
in January 1941.

171
00:06:55,125 --> 00:06:57,621
In April 1944, in New Guinea,

172
00:06:57,641 --> 00:06:59,760
it got a new commander, Lieutenant Colonel

173
00:06:59,780 --> 00:07:01,245
Henry Mucci of Bridgeport,

174
00:07:01,265 --> 00:07:05,705
Connecticut, a West Point graduate of 1936.

175
00:07:05,725 --> 00:07:07,665
He turned the gunners into Rangers.

176
00:07:09,025 --> 00:07:12,724
In September 1944 the unit became the

177
00:07:12,744 --> 00:07:14,224
Sixth Ranger Infantry Battalion.

178
00:07:14,244 --> 00:07:16,098
On the seventeenth of October,

179
00:07:16,118 --> 00:07:18,347
three days before MacArthur's landing on

180
00:07:18,367 --> 00:07:20,971
Leyte, the Rangers went ashore on the

181
00:07:20,991 --> 00:07:22,865
small islands guarding Leyte Gulf.

182
00:07:24,175 --> 00:07:26,550
For the camp, Mucci chose Company C,

183
00:07:26,570 --> 00:07:28,575
under a twenty-five-year-old captain from

184
00:07:28,595 --> 00:07:31,005
Seattle, Robert Prince, a Stanford graduate.

185
00:07:31,025 --> 00:07:34,245
He added the second platoon of Company F,

186
00:07:34,265 --> 00:07:36,270
under First Lieutenant John Murphy.

187
00:07:36,290 --> 00:07:39,510
With a few others and four Signal Corps

188
00:07:39,530 --> 00:07:41,940
combat photographers, the force came to

189
00:07:41,960 --> 00:07:44,775
eight officers and a hundred and twenty

190
00:07:44,795 --> 00:07:45,585
enlisted men.

191
00:07:45,605 --> 00:07:48,420
The newspapers would call them the hundred

192
00:07:48,440 --> 00:07:49,655
and twenty-one Rangers.

193
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,150
They went in soft caps and fatigues,

194
00:07:53,170 --> 00:07:55,320
with no insignia or badges of rank.

195
00:07:55,340 --> 00:07:59,124
Mucci himself took only a forty-five pistol.

196
00:07:59,144 --> 00:08:01,858
Years later, Prince said they all felt

197
00:08:01,878 --> 00:08:03,440
privileged to be chosen.

198
00:08:05,225 --> 00:08:08,107
Ahead of the Rangers went two teams of

199
00:08:08,127 --> 00:08:10,283
Alamo Scouts, Krueger's own reconnaissance men.

200
00:08:10,303 --> 00:08:12,822
They were small teams trained to slip

201
00:08:12,842 --> 00:08:14,273
behind Japanese lines, watch,

202
00:08:14,293 --> 00:08:15,361
and come back.

203
00:08:15,381 --> 00:08:18,263
The Army says they went through the whole

204
00:08:18,283 --> 00:08:21,185
war without a single man killed or captured.

205
00:08:22,475 --> 00:08:25,221
The two teams were led by First Lieutenant

206
00:08:25,241 --> 00:08:26,949
William Nellist and First Lieutenant

207
00:08:26,969 --> 00:08:29,933
Thomas Rounsaville, a young officer from Oklahoma.

208
00:08:29,953 --> 00:08:32,143
They left the American lines on the

209
00:08:32,162 --> 00:08:34,036
evening of the twenty-seventh of January,

210
00:08:34,056 --> 00:08:35,930
a day ahead of the Rangers,

211
00:08:35,950 --> 00:08:38,475
to find the camp and count the guards.

212
00:08:39,850 --> 00:08:43,008
Nellist found a way to watch the gate up close.

213
00:08:43,028 --> 00:08:45,550
He and one of his scouts, Private Rufo

214
00:08:45,570 --> 00:08:48,034
Vaquilar, a Filipino, put on local

215
00:08:48,054 --> 00:08:50,280
farmers' clothes and moved into an empty

216
00:08:50,300 --> 00:08:52,846
grass hut in a field facing the camp,

217
00:08:52,866 --> 00:08:55,412
a few hundred yards from the main gate.

218
00:08:55,432 --> 00:08:57,979
From there they could see the guards and

219
00:08:57,999 --> 00:08:59,583
the towers, and sketch them.

220
00:08:59,603 --> 00:09:01,508
On the afternoon of the raid,

221
00:09:01,528 --> 00:09:02,910
Nellist's sketch reached Mucci,

222
00:09:02,930 --> 00:09:04,990
with an aerial photograph of the camp.

223
00:09:06,650 --> 00:09:09,224
The Rangers left their base camp by truck

224
00:09:09,244 --> 00:09:11,493
at five in the morning on the

225
00:09:11,513 --> 00:09:12,466
twenty-eighth of January.

226
00:09:12,486 --> 00:09:15,235
At Guimba, on the edge of the American

227
00:09:15,255 --> 00:09:17,598
lines, they got down and started walking

228
00:09:17,618 --> 00:09:19,285
at two in the afternoon.

229
00:09:19,305 --> 00:09:21,985
At the village of Lobong they were joined

230
00:09:22,005 --> 00:09:23,672
by Filipino guerrillas under Captain

231
00:09:23,692 --> 00:09:25,750
Eduardo Joson, about eighty men.

232
00:09:27,050 --> 00:09:29,439
After dark they crossed into Japanese-held

233
00:09:29,459 --> 00:09:29,840
country.

234
00:09:29,860 --> 00:09:33,052
Late that night they had to lie hidden

235
00:09:33,072 --> 00:09:35,863
while Japanese trucks and tanks went by.

236
00:09:35,883 --> 00:09:38,968
At midnight they waded the Talavera River.

237
00:09:38,988 --> 00:09:41,830
At four in the morning they crossed the

238
00:09:41,850 --> 00:09:44,150
Rizal highway, the main road north from

239
00:09:44,170 --> 00:09:45,675
Cabanatuan.

240
00:09:46,375 --> 00:09:48,447
At six in the morning of the twenty-ninth

241
00:09:48,467 --> 00:09:50,675
they reached the village of Balincarin,

242
00:09:50,695 --> 00:09:53,046
after a fourteen-mile night march without

243
00:09:53,066 --> 00:09:53,837
being seen.

244
00:09:53,857 --> 00:09:56,999
There they met the Alamo Scouts and the

245
00:09:57,019 --> 00:09:58,185
local guerrilla commander,

246
00:09:58,205 --> 00:10:00,135
Captain Juan Pajota.

247
00:10:00,155 --> 00:10:03,752
Pajota had about ninety armed men and a

248
00:10:03,772 --> 00:10:05,507
hundred and sixty unarmed ones.

249
00:10:05,527 --> 00:10:08,335
He also brought news that changed the plan.

250
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:13,000
Juan Pajota was from Nueva Ecija,

251
00:10:13,020 --> 00:10:14,778
the province around the camp.

252
00:10:14,798 --> 00:10:17,267
He had trained with the Philippine Scouts

253
00:10:17,287 --> 00:10:19,400
and joined the guerrillas during the

254
00:10:19,420 --> 00:10:20,467
retreat to Bataan.

255
00:10:20,487 --> 00:10:23,311
His men knew the trails and the villages

256
00:10:23,331 --> 00:10:24,378
around the camp.

257
00:10:24,398 --> 00:10:27,100
And what he told Mucci was alarming.

258
00:10:28,475 --> 00:10:30,547
About a mile north of the camp,

259
00:10:30,567 --> 00:10:32,340
two to three hundred Japanese soldiers

260
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:34,138
were camped along the Cabu Creek.

261
00:10:34,158 --> 00:10:37,297
Later reports put the force at Cabu at

262
00:10:37,317 --> 00:10:39,711
about eight hundred men, with tanks.

263
00:10:39,731 --> 00:10:42,564
And in Cabanatuan City, less than four

264
00:10:42,584 --> 00:10:45,240
miles to the south, there was at least one

265
00:10:45,260 --> 00:10:46,060
Japanese division.

266
00:10:47,225 --> 00:10:49,026
Mucci made his decision.

267
00:10:49,046 --> 00:10:51,922
He postponed the raid for twenty-four hours.

268
00:10:51,942 --> 00:10:55,665
The extra day let the scouts finish their work.

269
00:10:56,875 --> 00:10:59,015
Mucci asked the guerrillas to gather a

270
00:10:59,035 --> 00:11:00,995
train of carabao carts, Filipino farm

271
00:11:01,015 --> 00:11:02,695
carts pulled by water buffalo,

272
00:11:02,715 --> 00:11:05,075
big enough to carry two hundred freed

273
00:11:05,095 --> 00:11:07,455
prisoners, and to cook food for six

274
00:11:07,475 --> 00:11:08,815
hundred and fifty men.

275
00:11:08,835 --> 00:11:11,535
He asked the villagers to pen up their

276
00:11:11,555 --> 00:11:14,255
chickens and to tie and muzzle their dogs,

277
00:11:14,275 --> 00:11:16,975
so no barking would give the column away.

278
00:11:16,995 --> 00:11:19,015
Captain Prince later said the Filipinos

279
00:11:19,035 --> 00:11:21,755
took care of at least five hundred dogs.

280
00:11:23,075 --> 00:11:25,015
That afternoon the Rangers moved to the

281
00:11:25,035 --> 00:11:27,555
village of Platero, closer to the camp,

282
00:11:27,575 --> 00:11:29,375
where the people welcomed them with a

283
00:11:29,395 --> 00:11:30,695
choir and a big meal.

284
00:11:32,400 --> 00:11:34,426
By the morning of the thirtieth,

285
00:11:34,446 --> 00:11:37,154
the scouts had drawn the camp in detail.

286
00:11:37,174 --> 00:11:39,882
It measured six hundred by eight hundred yards.

287
00:11:39,902 --> 00:11:41,928
The main gate, on the highway,

288
00:11:41,948 --> 00:11:45,338
was eight feet high and held by a heavy lock.

289
00:11:45,358 --> 00:11:47,043
There were three guard towers,

290
00:11:47,063 --> 00:11:50,112
twelve feet tall, and a pillbox with four men.

291
00:11:50,132 --> 00:11:52,860
The prisoners were kept in the northwest corner.

292
00:11:54,100 --> 00:11:55,813
There were seventy-three Japanese guards.

293
00:11:55,833 --> 00:11:57,547
But at eleven that morning,

294
00:11:57,567 --> 00:12:00,320
about a hundred and fifty more troops had

295
00:12:00,340 --> 00:12:03,460
marched into the compound to rest for the night.

296
00:12:04,725 --> 00:12:06,922
Captain Prince drew up the assault.

297
00:12:06,942 --> 00:12:09,508
His company would attack the main gate

298
00:12:09,528 --> 00:12:10,616
from the front.

299
00:12:10,636 --> 00:12:12,463
Lieutenant Murphy's platoon would circle

300
00:12:12,483 --> 00:12:15,049
around to the rear of the camp.

301
00:12:15,069 --> 00:12:17,635
Murphy's first shot would be the signal.

302
00:12:17,655 --> 00:12:19,482
When the prisoners were out,

303
00:12:19,502 --> 00:12:21,699
Prince would fire a red flare,

304
00:12:21,719 --> 00:12:24,285
and everyone would pull back to the

305
00:12:24,305 --> 00:12:25,105
Pampanga River.

306
00:12:26,400 --> 00:12:30,016
The guerrillas would hold the road on both sides.

307
00:12:30,036 --> 00:12:32,820
Captain Joson would block it eight hundred

308
00:12:32,840 --> 00:12:35,480
yards to the southwest, with a Ranger

309
00:12:35,500 --> 00:12:36,240
bazooka team.

310
00:12:36,260 --> 00:12:38,430
Captain Pajota would hold the bridge over

311
00:12:38,450 --> 00:12:41,199
the Cabu Creek, three hundred yards to the

312
00:12:41,219 --> 00:12:43,457
northeast, between the camp and the

313
00:12:43,477 --> 00:12:44,210
Japanese battalion.

314
00:12:44,230 --> 00:12:47,598
Under the far end of that bridge his men

315
00:12:47,618 --> 00:12:50,986
had placed a time bomb, which had come in

316
00:12:51,006 --> 00:12:52,116
by American submarine.

317
00:12:52,136 --> 00:12:55,127
It was set to go off between seven-forty

318
00:12:55,147 --> 00:12:55,947
and seven-fifty.

319
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:58,487
There was one problem left:

320
00:12:58,507 --> 00:12:59,390
the open field.

321
00:12:59,410 --> 00:13:02,403
For more than a mile in front of the gate

322
00:13:02,423 --> 00:13:04,513
there was nothing but flat ground and

323
00:13:04,533 --> 00:13:05,115
short grass.

324
00:13:05,135 --> 00:13:07,827
The Rangers would have to cross it in the

325
00:13:07,847 --> 00:13:09,936
last light, and every guard tower looked

326
00:13:09,956 --> 00:13:10,860
straight at it.

327
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:15,120
The answer came from Pajota.

328
00:13:15,140 --> 00:13:16,702
According to the Army's study,

329
00:13:16,722 --> 00:13:18,917
it was on his recommendation that an

330
00:13:18,937 --> 00:13:21,132
American plane would fly over the camp

331
00:13:21,152 --> 00:13:23,663
just before the attack, to pull every eye

332
00:13:23,683 --> 00:13:25,878
in the camp up into the sky.

333
00:13:25,898 --> 00:13:27,776
The Special Operations history gives part

334
00:13:27,796 --> 00:13:30,307
of the credit to a Sixth Army planner,

335
00:13:30,327 --> 00:13:32,000
Major Frank Rowale.

336
00:13:32,020 --> 00:13:34,862
The night fighters of the Five Hundred and

337
00:13:34,882 --> 00:13:36,651
Forty-Seventh Night Fighter Squadron were

338
00:13:36,671 --> 00:13:38,460
briefed directly by Colonel White.

339
00:13:39,750 --> 00:13:40,982
Prince had his doubts.

340
00:13:41,002 --> 00:13:43,798
He later said he did not think the aerial

341
00:13:43,818 --> 00:13:45,988
decoy would work, and later still called

342
00:13:46,008 --> 00:13:48,491
the plane one of the biggest factors in

343
00:13:48,511 --> 00:13:49,450
keeping the surprise.

344
00:13:50,825 --> 00:13:53,182
At five in the afternoon of the thirtieth

345
00:13:53,202 --> 00:13:56,605
of January, the whole force left Platero.

346
00:13:56,625 --> 00:13:58,765
With the guerrillas it numbered nearly

347
00:13:58,785 --> 00:14:00,565
three hundred and seventy-five men.

348
00:14:00,585 --> 00:14:04,147
Mucci's last order is quoted from his own

349
00:14:04,167 --> 00:14:06,345
article of 1945:

350
00:14:06,365 --> 00:14:08,885
all of the prisoners go, no one is left behind.

351
00:14:10,225 --> 00:14:12,788
If you want more prisoner of war stories

352
00:14:12,808 --> 00:14:15,505
told from the documents, subscribe to WWII

353
00:14:15,525 --> 00:14:15,849
Frontlines.

354
00:14:15,869 --> 00:14:19,305
Now, back to the field in front of the gate.

355
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:22,938
At six o'clock, in the twilight,

356
00:14:22,958 --> 00:14:25,875
the Rangers reached the edge of the open plain.

357
00:14:25,895 --> 00:14:28,486
Prince's two platoons went down on their bellies.

358
00:14:28,506 --> 00:14:31,423
The camp was a mile away, and they would

359
00:14:31,443 --> 00:14:33,707
have to crawl for seventy-five minutes to

360
00:14:33,727 --> 00:14:34,527
reach it.

361
00:14:35,550 --> 00:14:38,123
At twenty to seven the plane arrived.

362
00:14:38,143 --> 00:14:41,458
It was a P-61 Black Widow named Hard to

363
00:14:41,478 --> 00:14:43,710
Get, flown by Captain Kenneth Schrieber

364
00:14:43,730 --> 00:14:46,623
with Lieutenant Bonnie Rucks as radar operator.

365
00:14:46,643 --> 00:14:49,953
It came over the bridge and the camp

366
00:14:49,973 --> 00:14:51,201
twice, very low.

367
00:14:51,221 --> 00:14:53,895
One account says Schrieber cut one engine

368
00:14:53,915 --> 00:14:56,969
over the camp and started it again so that

369
00:14:56,989 --> 00:14:58,677
it backfired like a gun.

370
00:14:58,697 --> 00:15:00,727
Prince remembered him cutting his motor

371
00:15:00,747 --> 00:15:03,460
and doing every crazy thing he could to

372
00:15:03,480 --> 00:15:04,143
attract attention.

373
00:15:04,163 --> 00:15:06,192
Lieutenant Nellist, watching from his hut,

374
00:15:06,212 --> 00:15:08,584
said the plane just about ripped the

375
00:15:08,604 --> 00:15:09,970
shingles off the camp.

376
00:15:11,250 --> 00:15:11,989
It worked.

377
00:15:12,009 --> 00:15:13,887
The guards watched the plane.

378
00:15:13,907 --> 00:15:16,165
Murphy's platoon slipped under the highway

379
00:15:16,185 --> 00:15:18,822
through a large culvert and worked around

380
00:15:18,842 --> 00:15:19,961
to the rear.

381
00:15:19,981 --> 00:15:22,997
Prince's men crawled up into a ditch about

382
00:15:23,017 --> 00:15:25,275
twenty yards from the main gate.

383
00:15:25,295 --> 00:15:26,793
At twenty-five past seven,

384
00:15:26,813 --> 00:15:29,850
the last of Murphy's men were in position.

385
00:15:31,200 --> 00:15:32,673
The plan said seven-thirty.

386
00:15:32,693 --> 00:15:35,287
Murphy took fifteen more minutes to check

387
00:15:35,307 --> 00:15:36,033
his men.

388
00:15:36,053 --> 00:15:39,020
The moon was up, and it was bright.

389
00:15:39,040 --> 00:15:42,380
The Rangers could see the red tips of the

390
00:15:42,400 --> 00:15:44,620
guards' cigarettes, and men walking around

391
00:15:44,640 --> 00:15:45,760
in their underwear.

392
00:15:47,450 --> 00:15:49,715
At a quarter to eight, Lieutenant Murphy

393
00:15:49,735 --> 00:15:52,654
aimed his M-1 rifle at an open window in

394
00:15:52,674 --> 00:15:54,286
the nearest barracks and fired.

395
00:15:54,306 --> 00:15:56,898
The Army's study puts it in one line:

396
00:15:56,918 --> 00:15:58,550
his shot was the signal.

397
00:15:59,850 --> 00:16:02,073
Every Ranger opened fire at once.

398
00:16:02,093 --> 00:16:03,942
According to the Army's study,

399
00:16:03,962 --> 00:16:06,558
all the guard towers, guard shacks and

400
00:16:06,578 --> 00:16:08,801
pillboxes were silenced within thirty seconds.

401
00:16:08,821 --> 00:16:11,418
At the main gate, Staff Sergeant Theodore

402
00:16:11,438 --> 00:16:14,035
Richardson of Dallas shot the heavy lock

403
00:16:14,055 --> 00:16:15,530
off with his pistol.

404
00:16:15,550 --> 00:16:17,399
In the War Department's account,

405
00:16:17,419 --> 00:16:20,763
the pistol was then shot out of his hand.

406
00:16:20,783 --> 00:16:23,380
He said a Japanese soldier asked him,

407
00:16:23,400 --> 00:16:24,875
in perfectly good English,

408
00:16:24,895 --> 00:16:26,390
what was going on.

409
00:16:27,600 --> 00:16:29,186
The Rangers poured in.

410
00:16:29,206 --> 00:16:31,594
A bazooka section under Staff Sergeant

411
00:16:31,614 --> 00:16:34,805
Manton Stewart blew up two trucks and a

412
00:16:34,825 --> 00:16:37,614
metal shed where the Japanese kept tanks.

413
00:16:37,634 --> 00:16:39,900
Private First Class Leland Provencher

414
00:16:39,920 --> 00:16:41,202
reached the first prisoner,

415
00:16:41,222 --> 00:16:43,500
a man who ran the camp's generator.

416
00:16:44,875 --> 00:16:47,448
Inside the prisoners' huts there was panic.

417
00:16:47,468 --> 00:16:50,412
Some were afraid it was a Japanese trick.

418
00:16:50,432 --> 00:16:52,635
A Navy warrant officer, Paul Jackson,

419
00:16:52,655 --> 00:16:55,228
wrote two months later that when the

420
00:16:55,248 --> 00:16:57,821
firing started, most of them thought the

421
00:16:57,841 --> 00:17:00,044
Japanese were coming to kill them.

422
00:17:00,064 --> 00:17:02,931
A marine from Corregidor, Corporal Milton

423
00:17:02,951 --> 00:17:05,159
Englin, said he thought the rescuers were

424
00:17:05,179 --> 00:17:07,387
guerrillas, until some big Texan told him

425
00:17:07,407 --> 00:17:09,296
to head for the main gate.

426
00:17:09,316 --> 00:17:11,842
The prisoner who had been taking off his

427
00:17:11,862 --> 00:17:13,135
shoes had another problem.

428
00:17:14,425 --> 00:17:16,905
I can't see very well at night.

429
00:17:16,925 --> 00:17:20,476
In fact, I can't see at all when it's dark.

430
00:17:20,496 --> 00:17:23,691
And one of the Rangers grabbed a hold of

431
00:17:23,711 --> 00:17:26,925
my arm and he led me through the camp.

432
00:17:27,625 --> 00:17:30,131
Prince said they found two hundred and

433
00:17:30,151 --> 00:17:32,657
twenty-five men who had to be carried.

434
00:17:32,677 --> 00:17:35,184
Some Rangers took off their socks and

435
00:17:35,204 --> 00:17:38,071
their shoes and gave them to barefoot prisoners.

436
00:17:38,091 --> 00:17:40,597
A Ranger sergeant said the prisoners who

437
00:17:40,617 --> 00:17:43,484
could walk wanted to carry the Rangers' rifles.

438
00:17:43,504 --> 00:17:46,095
And Mucci wrote about one old man who told

439
00:17:46,115 --> 00:17:46,365
him:

440
00:17:46,385 --> 00:17:48,255
I made the death march from Bataan,

441
00:17:48,275 --> 00:17:50,165
and I can certainly make this one.

442
00:17:51,400 --> 00:17:53,880
The British prisoners had their own answer.

443
00:17:53,900 --> 00:17:56,188
Mucci heard them shout:

444
00:17:56,208 --> 00:17:58,420
we're not Americans, but we're coming too.

445
00:18:00,100 --> 00:18:02,256
At the same moment, three hundred yards up

446
00:18:02,276 --> 00:18:04,498
the road, Pajota's fight began.

447
00:18:04,518 --> 00:18:07,370
When the first shots came from the camp,

448
00:18:07,390 --> 00:18:09,884
his men opened fire on the Japanese

449
00:18:09,904 --> 00:18:11,943
battalion camped beyond the Cabu Creek.

450
00:18:11,963 --> 00:18:14,974
The time bomb went off and blew a gap in

451
00:18:14,994 --> 00:18:15,794
the bridge.

452
00:18:16,950 --> 00:18:20,710
Pajota had kept a secret from Mucci.

453
00:18:20,730 --> 00:18:23,194
Besides the men he had shown the Rangers,

454
00:18:23,214 --> 00:18:25,368
he had quietly sent about four hundred

455
00:18:25,388 --> 00:18:26,920
more guerrillas ahead, with four

456
00:18:26,940 --> 00:18:27,852
water-cooled machine guns.

457
00:18:27,872 --> 00:18:30,336
Now they were all on the creek bank,

458
00:18:30,356 --> 00:18:33,150
firing into the Japanese as they tried to cross.

459
00:18:34,475 --> 00:18:36,486
Ranger bazooka men posted with the

460
00:18:36,506 --> 00:18:39,195
guerrillas knocked out two tanks and a truck.

461
00:18:39,215 --> 00:18:43,137
Pajota's men held the bridge until a

462
00:18:43,157 --> 00:18:45,105
little after ten that night.

463
00:18:45,125 --> 00:18:47,467
The Army's study says his guerrillas

464
00:18:47,487 --> 00:18:49,455
virtually destroyed a Japanese battalion.

465
00:18:51,300 --> 00:18:53,425
About twelve minutes into the attack,

466
00:18:53,445 --> 00:18:56,284
the shooting in the camp was down to

467
00:18:56,304 --> 00:18:56,999
scattered shots.

468
00:18:57,019 --> 00:18:59,501
Then a Japanese light mortar fired three

469
00:18:59,521 --> 00:19:01,288
rounds toward the main gate,

470
00:19:01,308 --> 00:19:03,790
where the Rangers were gathering the prisoners.

471
00:19:03,810 --> 00:19:05,240
Six men were wounded.

472
00:19:06,575 --> 00:19:08,622
Two of them were officers.

473
00:19:08,642 --> 00:19:11,515
One was the Alamo Scout Lieutenant Tom

474
00:19:11,535 --> 00:19:13,035
Rounsaville.

475
00:19:13,055 --> 00:19:15,305
The other was the battalion surgeon,

476
00:19:15,325 --> 00:19:16,439
Captain James Fisher.

477
00:19:16,459 --> 00:19:18,331
Fisher was a Harvard-trained doctor,

478
00:19:18,351 --> 00:19:20,979
the son of the American author Dorothy

479
00:19:20,999 --> 00:19:21,735
Canfield Fisher.

480
00:19:21,755 --> 00:19:24,005
The Rangers called him Doctor Jimmy.

481
00:19:24,025 --> 00:19:27,031
He had argued to go forward with the

482
00:19:27,051 --> 00:19:30,057
assault company instead of staying at the rear.

483
00:19:30,077 --> 00:19:33,084
Now he lay at the gate, bleeding badly

484
00:19:33,104 --> 00:19:34,995
from wounds to the liver.

485
00:19:36,300 --> 00:19:39,233
At a quarter past eight, Captain Prince

486
00:19:39,253 --> 00:19:40,920
fired the red flare.

487
00:19:40,940 --> 00:19:42,185
The withdrawal began.

488
00:19:42,205 --> 00:19:44,716
As the last Rangers pulled back,

489
00:19:44,736 --> 00:19:47,695
Corporal Roy Sweezy turned to fire at the

490
00:19:47,715 --> 00:19:50,152
Japanese and was shot in the chest.

491
00:19:50,172 --> 00:19:52,258
He died a few minutes later.

492
00:19:52,278 --> 00:19:55,066
The accounts disagree about who fired that shot.

493
00:19:55,086 --> 00:19:57,874
He and Captain Fisher would be the only

494
00:19:57,894 --> 00:20:00,000
Rangers to die in the operation.

495
00:20:01,400 --> 00:20:04,354
One prisoner died of a heart attack as he

496
00:20:04,374 --> 00:20:06,666
was being helped out of the camp.

497
00:20:06,686 --> 00:20:08,318
And one was left behind.

498
00:20:08,338 --> 00:20:10,631
In the confusion, a sick British prisoner

499
00:20:10,651 --> 00:20:12,613
had fallen asleep in the latrine.

500
00:20:12,633 --> 00:20:14,926
He woke up to an empty camp.

501
00:20:14,946 --> 00:20:16,908
Filipino guerrillas found him later that

502
00:20:16,928 --> 00:20:18,560
night, or the next morning;

503
00:20:18,580 --> 00:20:20,543
the accounts differ on that too,

504
00:20:20,563 --> 00:20:23,516
and in one telling, he took the time to

505
00:20:23,536 --> 00:20:26,159
shave and put on his best clothes before

506
00:20:26,179 --> 00:20:26,979
walking out.

507
00:20:28,450 --> 00:20:30,672
By half past eight most of the column had

508
00:20:30,692 --> 00:20:32,084
reached the Pampanga River,

509
00:20:32,104 --> 00:20:35,813
and by a quarter to nine everyone was across.

510
00:20:35,833 --> 00:20:38,713
A second flare told the guerrillas the

511
00:20:38,733 --> 00:20:39,956
Rangers were clear.

512
00:20:39,976 --> 00:20:42,380
At Platero, a Filipino doctor,

513
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:44,361
Carlos Layug, and his wife,

514
00:20:44,381 --> 00:20:46,735
also a doctor, had set up an aid station

515
00:20:46,755 --> 00:20:47,810
in an empty schoolhouse.

516
00:20:49,100 --> 00:20:50,582
Then came the carts.

517
00:20:50,602 --> 00:20:53,960
About a hundred and fifteen men went on to

518
00:20:53,980 --> 00:20:56,830
Balincarin in twenty-five carabao carts.

519
00:20:56,850 --> 00:20:58,927
There fifteen more carts were waiting.

520
00:20:58,947 --> 00:21:01,723
At two in the morning, at another village,

521
00:21:01,743 --> 00:21:02,423
eleven more.

522
00:21:02,443 --> 00:21:05,219
When the column left there at half past

523
00:21:05,239 --> 00:21:08,365
two, it had fifty-one carts and was a mile

524
00:21:08,385 --> 00:21:09,763
and a half long.

525
00:21:09,783 --> 00:21:12,920
By the time it reached the town of Sibul

526
00:21:12,940 --> 00:21:14,912
in the morning, there were seventy-one

527
00:21:14,932 --> 00:21:17,060
carts, three times what Mucci had planned.

528
00:21:18,425 --> 00:21:21,163
The most dangerous part was the Rizal highway.

529
00:21:21,183 --> 00:21:24,569
The column had to march along it for a

530
00:21:24,589 --> 00:21:26,839
mile, in the open, while guerrilla

531
00:21:26,859 --> 00:21:28,731
roadblocks watched both directions for

532
00:21:28,751 --> 00:21:29,488
Japanese traffic.

533
00:21:29,508 --> 00:21:30,244
None came.

534
00:21:30,264 --> 00:21:33,650
The last man was across by half past four

535
00:21:33,670 --> 00:21:34,805
in the morning.

536
00:21:36,100 --> 00:21:38,323
The Rangers walked beside the carts the

537
00:21:38,343 --> 00:21:38,963
whole night.

538
00:21:38,983 --> 00:21:41,206
The Navy warrant officer Paul Jackson had

539
00:21:41,226 --> 00:21:43,128
lost a leg in the camp.

540
00:21:43,148 --> 00:21:46,012
He wrote that after half a mile on his

541
00:21:46,032 --> 00:21:49,216
homemade peg he had to give up and let his

542
00:21:49,236 --> 00:21:50,177
rescuers carry him.

543
00:21:50,197 --> 00:21:52,740
And he wrote that the Rangers pushed the

544
00:21:52,760 --> 00:21:54,250
carabao carts through the night,

545
00:21:54,270 --> 00:21:55,400
and never a complaint.

546
00:21:57,150 --> 00:22:00,622
Captain Fisher could not be moved with the column.

547
00:22:00,642 --> 00:22:02,570
At Balincarin, Dr.

548
00:22:02,590 --> 00:22:05,861
Layug and one of the freed prisoners,

549
00:22:05,881 --> 00:22:07,453
a surgeon named Merle Musselman,

550
00:22:07,473 --> 00:22:08,409
operated on him.

551
00:22:08,429 --> 00:22:10,957
When he needed blood, an Alamo Scout gave

552
00:22:10,977 --> 00:22:13,823
it to him, straight from one man's vein to

553
00:22:13,843 --> 00:22:14,460
the other's.

554
00:22:14,480 --> 00:22:16,690
The scouts cleared a rough landing strip

555
00:22:16,710 --> 00:22:19,556
in a field and a light plane was requested

556
00:22:19,576 --> 00:22:20,850
to fly him out.

557
00:22:22,075 --> 00:22:23,888
The plane never came.

558
00:22:23,908 --> 00:22:27,097
James Fisher died on the thirty-first of

559
00:22:27,117 --> 00:22:29,847
January, about fifteen hours after the

560
00:22:29,867 --> 00:22:30,763
raid began.

561
00:22:30,783 --> 00:22:34,715
Filipino guerrillas buried him near Platero.

562
00:22:34,735 --> 00:22:36,686
One account gives his last words as:

563
00:22:36,706 --> 00:22:38,395
good luck on the way out.

564
00:22:39,600 --> 00:22:42,071
His body was later moved to the Manila

565
00:22:42,091 --> 00:22:43,960
American Cemetery, where he lies today.

566
00:22:45,650 --> 00:22:48,579
At Sibul, at eight in the morning,

567
00:22:48,599 --> 00:22:51,279
the Rangers finally got through to Sixth

568
00:22:51,299 --> 00:22:52,436
Army by radio.

569
00:22:52,456 --> 00:22:55,907
Around eleven, a Ranger at the head of the

570
00:22:55,927 --> 00:22:58,607
column, Patrick Marquis, met an American patrol.

571
00:22:58,627 --> 00:23:00,536
Trucks and ambulances came up.

572
00:23:00,556 --> 00:23:03,621
An hour later the freed prisoners were at

573
00:23:03,641 --> 00:23:06,625
the Ninety-Second Evacuation Hospital at Guimba.

574
00:23:07,325 --> 00:23:09,625
Staff Sergeant Clinton Goodbla,

575
00:23:09,645 --> 00:23:11,385
a coast artilleryman captured on

576
00:23:11,405 --> 00:23:13,689
Corregidor, told an Associated Press

577
00:23:13,709 --> 00:23:16,496
reporter what it was like to walk through

578
00:23:16,516 --> 00:23:17,900
the lines that morning.

579
00:23:17,920 --> 00:23:20,356
It cut right through me, he said.

580
00:23:20,376 --> 00:23:22,812
The flag, it cut right through me.

581
00:23:22,832 --> 00:23:24,918
Being free cut right through me.

582
00:23:24,938 --> 00:23:27,023
A corporal from the same camp,

583
00:23:27,043 --> 00:23:29,830
Cecil Hay, told a newsreel camera the same

584
00:23:29,850 --> 00:23:31,605
thing in his own words.

585
00:23:32,875 --> 00:23:37,458
You people do not realize how happy us

586
00:23:37,478 --> 00:23:40,911
boys were when we were liberated.

587
00:23:40,931 --> 00:23:44,364
I didn't realize myself until we...

588
00:23:44,384 --> 00:23:47,816
saw the flag that was flying,

589
00:23:47,836 --> 00:23:53,015
and then I had to break down and cry.

590
00:23:53,875 --> 00:23:56,086
General Krueger came to see them.

591
00:23:56,106 --> 00:23:59,433
He wrote later that most of them were in

592
00:23:59,453 --> 00:24:01,664
pitiable condition and could not realize

593
00:24:01,684 --> 00:24:03,152
they were actually safe.

594
00:24:03,172 --> 00:24:06,498
Some had lost as much as a hundred pounds.

595
00:24:06,518 --> 00:24:08,730
A British signalman captured at Singapore,

596
00:24:08,750 --> 00:24:10,961
Thomas Potter, tasted white bread again.

597
00:24:10,981 --> 00:24:12,820
Blimey, he told a reporter,

598
00:24:12,840 --> 00:24:15,815
this is better than I got in peacetime.

599
00:24:17,525 --> 00:24:19,375
On the first of February, General

600
00:24:19,395 --> 00:24:21,645
MacArthur came to the hospital at Guimba

601
00:24:21,665 --> 00:24:25,765
and greeted the men of Bataan and Corregidor.

602
00:24:25,785 --> 00:24:28,036
In a special communiqué he said:

603
00:24:28,056 --> 00:24:31,063
no incident of the campaign has given me

604
00:24:31,083 --> 00:24:32,198
such personal satisfaction.

605
00:24:32,218 --> 00:24:34,469
He awarded the commander the Distinguished

606
00:24:34,489 --> 00:24:36,739
Service Cross, every other officer the

607
00:24:36,759 --> 00:24:39,388
Silver Star, and every enlisted man the

608
00:24:39,408 --> 00:24:40,208
Bronze Star.

609
00:24:41,450 --> 00:24:44,108
The story hit the front pages of America

610
00:24:44,128 --> 00:24:46,452
on the first and second of February.

611
00:24:46,472 --> 00:24:48,795
The Associated Press wrote that men of

612
00:24:48,815 --> 00:24:50,919
Bataan, Corregidor and Singapore,

613
00:24:50,939 --> 00:24:52,985
five hundred and thirteen of them,

614
00:24:53,005 --> 00:24:55,740
had been snatched from the Japanese in an

615
00:24:55,760 --> 00:24:57,118
exploit of unmatched daring.

616
00:24:57,138 --> 00:24:59,184
Another reporter wrote of a long,

617
00:24:59,204 --> 00:25:01,595
dirty, twisting lane that should become a

618
00:25:01,615 --> 00:25:04,370
war monument, because it had bridged two worlds.

619
00:25:05,675 --> 00:25:09,457
Not everything in those first reports was right.

620
00:25:09,477 --> 00:25:12,309
The first Associated Press story said

621
00:25:12,329 --> 00:25:15,160
twenty-seven Rangers and Filipinos had died.

622
00:25:15,180 --> 00:25:18,487
The Rangers' own log, written on the

623
00:25:18,507 --> 00:25:20,388
thirty-first of January, says:

624
00:25:20,408 --> 00:25:21,339
mission completed.

625
00:25:21,359 --> 00:25:21,814
Casualties:

626
00:25:21,834 --> 00:25:24,657
Captain Fisher and Corporal Sweezy killed

627
00:25:24,677 --> 00:25:25,582
in action.

628
00:25:25,602 --> 00:25:28,375
Five hundred and ten prisoners released.

629
00:25:29,700 --> 00:25:32,168
Even the count of the freed men never

630
00:25:32,188 --> 00:25:32,479
settled:

631
00:25:32,499 --> 00:25:34,656
five hundred and ten in the log,

632
00:25:34,676 --> 00:25:36,833
five hundred and eleven in the Army's

633
00:25:36,853 --> 00:25:39,321
study, five hundred and sixteen in a later

634
00:25:39,341 --> 00:25:40,565
count from the rosters.

635
00:25:40,585 --> 00:25:43,053
What is not in doubt is the cost:

636
00:25:43,073 --> 00:25:44,919
two Rangers dead, and the Japanese,

637
00:25:44,939 --> 00:25:47,096
by the Army's estimate, more than five

638
00:25:47,116 --> 00:25:48,360
hundred killed or wounded.

639
00:25:50,075 --> 00:25:52,526
Within two weeks, Army and newsreel

640
00:25:52,546 --> 00:25:54,996
cameramen were filming the freed men.

641
00:25:55,016 --> 00:25:58,135
A Universal newsreel of February 1945

642
00:25:58,155 --> 00:26:01,565
carried their words to theaters across America.

643
00:26:01,585 --> 00:26:04,035
This is Sergeant Samuel Goldie.

644
00:26:05,225 --> 00:26:06,469
Well, we're finally free Americans.

645
00:26:06,489 --> 00:26:08,492
It took them three years, but we're sure

646
00:26:08,512 --> 00:26:10,008
glad to get out of there.

647
00:26:10,028 --> 00:26:11,525
The Rangers came in that night,

648
00:26:11,545 --> 00:26:14,053
and it was about as happy as I had ever

649
00:26:14,073 --> 00:26:15,065
seen in my life.

650
00:26:15,085 --> 00:26:16,834
For three years, we couldn't do anything

651
00:26:16,854 --> 00:26:18,604
without someone telling us what to do.

652
00:26:18,624 --> 00:26:21,132
And now, at last, we can do what we want

653
00:26:21,152 --> 00:26:22,143
when we want to.

654
00:26:22,163 --> 00:26:24,418
We're sure glad to get back to the United

655
00:26:24,438 --> 00:26:26,188
States, where I'll be a free American

656
00:26:26,208 --> 00:26:27,725
again, and get myself a milkshake.

657
00:26:28,925 --> 00:26:30,586
On the eleventh of February,

658
00:26:30,606 --> 00:26:33,276
about two hundred and eighty of the freed

659
00:26:33,296 --> 00:26:35,294
prisoners sailed from Leyte for home.

660
00:26:35,314 --> 00:26:37,648
They reached San Francisco Bay on the

661
00:26:37,668 --> 00:26:38,656
eighth of March.

662
00:26:38,676 --> 00:26:41,683
The day before, a dozen of the raiders had

663
00:26:41,702 --> 00:26:44,373
been taken into the Oval Office to meet

664
00:26:44,393 --> 00:26:45,192
President Roosevelt.

665
00:26:46,425 --> 00:26:48,326
Captain Prince and eleven other raiders

666
00:26:48,346 --> 00:26:50,568
toured American war plants that spring to

667
00:26:50,588 --> 00:26:52,810
tell the workers what they had done.

668
00:26:52,830 --> 00:26:55,051
The War Department printed what they said.

669
00:26:55,071 --> 00:26:56,973
Prince told them the Filipinos were

670
00:26:56,993 --> 00:26:57,613
wonderful people:

671
00:26:57,633 --> 00:27:00,815
they fed us, fought with us and died for us.

672
00:27:00,835 --> 00:27:04,018
And he said that when he saw the camp in

673
00:27:04,038 --> 00:27:05,939
daylight three days after the raid,

674
00:27:05,959 --> 00:27:07,220
it looked much bigger.

675
00:27:07,240 --> 00:27:09,782
If he had seen it before, he said,

676
00:27:09,802 --> 00:27:12,984
he would not have had as much nerve as he

677
00:27:13,004 --> 00:27:13,965
did that night.

678
00:27:15,225 --> 00:27:17,234
Sixth Army's intelligence report called

679
00:27:17,254 --> 00:27:20,074
the raid an almost perfect example of

680
00:27:20,094 --> 00:27:21,696
prior reconnaissance and planning.

681
00:27:21,716 --> 00:27:24,536
An Army historian later called it perhaps

682
00:27:24,556 --> 00:27:26,971
the best conceived and executed Ranger

683
00:27:26,991 --> 00:27:29,425
mission of the Second World War.

684
00:27:31,100 --> 00:27:33,979
Henry Mucci was promoted to colonel and

685
00:27:33,999 --> 00:27:35,594
given an infantry regiment.

686
00:27:35,614 --> 00:27:39,240
He ran for Congress in Connecticut in 1946

687
00:27:39,260 --> 00:27:42,880
and lost, left the Army in 1947,

688
00:27:42,900 --> 00:27:45,824
and later worked in the oil business in India.

689
00:27:45,844 --> 00:27:48,100
He died in Florida in 1997,

690
00:27:48,120 --> 00:27:50,940
at eighty-eight, and is buried at West Point.

691
00:27:52,275 --> 00:27:54,875
Robert Prince left the Army as a major in

692
00:27:54,895 --> 00:27:58,437
1946 and spent some forty years in the

693
00:27:58,457 --> 00:28:00,367
apple business in Washington State.

694
00:28:00,387 --> 00:28:05,615
His son Steve was killed in Vietnam in 1969.

695
00:28:05,635 --> 00:28:07,906
Prince died on New Year's Day,

696
00:28:07,926 --> 00:28:09,835
2009, at Port Townsend, Washington.

697
00:28:11,225 --> 00:28:13,662
Tom Rounsaville recovered from his wound,

698
00:28:13,682 --> 00:28:16,282
fought in Korea and rose to colonel.

699
00:28:16,302 --> 00:28:20,645
He died in 1999 and is buried at Fort Benning.

700
00:28:20,665 --> 00:28:23,632
The Alamo Scout Galen Kittleson took part

701
00:28:23,652 --> 00:28:27,065
in four prisoner rescue operations in two wars.

702
00:28:28,325 --> 00:28:31,005
Eduardo Joson became governor of Nueva

703
00:28:31,025 --> 00:28:33,658
Ecija, the province of the raid,

704
00:28:33,678 --> 00:28:35,205
and served six terms.

705
00:28:35,225 --> 00:28:38,930
A hospital in Cabanatuan bears his name.

706
00:28:38,950 --> 00:28:41,973
Juan Pajota moved to the United States.

707
00:28:41,993 --> 00:28:45,155
He died of a heart attack in Chicago in

708
00:28:45,175 --> 00:28:48,338
the 1970s, days before he was to become an

709
00:28:48,358 --> 00:28:49,158
American citizen.

710
00:28:50,500 --> 00:28:53,350
Roy Sweezy and James Fisher lie in the

711
00:28:53,370 --> 00:28:55,741
Manila American Cemetery, among the men of

712
00:28:55,761 --> 00:28:57,449
the war in the Pacific.

713
00:28:57,469 --> 00:28:59,840
The Filipino guerrillas who fought at the

714
00:28:59,860 --> 00:29:02,203
Cabu bridge received the Bronze Star,

715
00:29:02,223 --> 00:29:04,287
like the American enlisted men.

716
00:29:04,307 --> 00:29:07,640
Their losses that night were never firmly counted.

717
00:29:09,325 --> 00:29:11,510
If your father, grandfather or uncle was

718
00:29:11,530 --> 00:29:13,864
at Cabanatuan, as a prisoner,

719
00:29:13,884 --> 00:29:15,888
a Ranger, an Alamo Scout or a guerrilla,

720
00:29:15,908 --> 00:29:18,165
or was one of the men of Bataan and

721
00:29:18,185 --> 00:29:21,515
Corregidor, tell us his name and his story

722
00:29:21,535 --> 00:29:22,454
in the comments.

723
00:29:22,474 --> 00:29:23,725
We read every one.

724
00:29:25,375 --> 00:29:27,014
The camp is gone.

725
00:29:27,034 --> 00:29:29,503
Pacific Wrecks reports that the only

726
00:29:29,523 --> 00:29:31,577
physical remains are six concrete

727
00:29:31,597 --> 00:29:34,481
foundation blocks from the camp's water tower.

728
00:29:34,501 --> 00:29:37,875
On the site stands the Cabanatuan American

729
00:29:37,895 --> 00:29:40,535
Memorial, built by the survivors of Bataan

730
00:29:40,555 --> 00:29:45,175
and the prison camp and dedicated in 1982.

731
00:29:45,195 --> 00:29:47,855
Its wall carries the names of two thousand

732
00:29:47,875 --> 00:29:49,865
six hundred and fifty-six Americans who

733
00:29:49,885 --> 00:29:50,685
died there.

734
00:29:51,750 --> 00:29:53,746
On the first of February, 2025,

735
00:29:53,766 --> 00:29:55,426
eighty years after the raid,

736
00:29:55,446 --> 00:29:57,106
American and Filipino soldiers stood

737
00:29:57,126 --> 00:29:58,450
together at that wall.

738
00:29:58,470 --> 00:30:00,466
Among them were Rangers of the

739
00:30:00,486 --> 00:30:01,474
Seventy-Fifth Ranger Regiment,

740
00:30:01,494 --> 00:30:04,162
who carry the lineage of the men who

741
00:30:04,182 --> 00:30:05,506
crawled across that field.

742
00:30:05,526 --> 00:30:08,194
Around them, the rice land is still flat

743
00:30:08,214 --> 00:30:11,554
and open, the way it was on the night a

744
00:30:11,574 --> 00:30:13,570
plane flew low over the camp,

745
00:30:13,590 --> 00:30:15,270
and every guard looked up.

